{"response": "In a valley where rivers braided like silver threads, there stood a lone tree, ancient as the first dawn. Its roots sank deep into the earth, its branches stretched toward the stars, and from its trunk hung a bell carved from rain and memory. The villagers said the bell did not ring with sound but with time itself, tolling when a heart learned a truth.

One winter, a girl named Luma wandered to the tree, eyes bright with questions. She asked the bell why the world felt so fragile, why the days wore thorny edges. The bell did not answer with words but with a vision. It showed her a child who believed the world would end when the last leaf fell. The child cried, and the wind gathered the tears, turning them to frost that kissed the hills and the roofs, and the world did not end, but slowed.

Luma understood: fear makes days narrow; courage widens them. She asked how to mend what broke. The bell tolled softly, and the vision shifted to a chorus of people planting seeds, mending nets, sharing bread, and telling stories by the fire. The valley learned that every act of kindness becomes a new root in the old tree, growing toward a future that holds all endings as beginnings."}

